## INTRODUCTION
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) constitute one of the most extensively studied subsets of porous materials in contemporary chemistry because MOFs can translate their modular topology and extraordinarily broad chemical variety into the tunability of their physicochemical functions.1-4  This tunability has accelerated the investigation particularly for various applications, including gas uptake,5-10 molecular separation,4,10-11 drug delivery,12-13 and catalysis.5,14-15  For sustainable utilization of MOFs in real-system, their long-term stability should be ensured.  However, most MOFs except only a few including highly oxidized metal ions with high coordination number such as $Z\mathrm{{r}^{\mathrm{{IV}}}}$ , in MOFs such as UiO-66,16- 21 have shown limitations in such applications due to low stability, especially when exposed to water or challenged under moist air.  In particular, MOFs possessing open-metal sites $(\mathrm{OMSs})^{22-25}$ have often characterized negatively in terms of hydrolytic stability,26-27 although such MOFs are expected to show high potential in a variety of catalytic reactions and chemisorptions.  For example, the applications of HKUST-1 (also known as $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ , where BTC3 is 1,3,5- benzenetricarboxylate)28 have been limited due to low hydrolytic stability although its chemistry is ideal for molecular uptake owing to the presence of OMS at paddlewheel $\mathrm{{Cu-Cu}}$ node.29-30
The hydrolytic stability of MOFs is primarily interpreted with the high stability of metal node, which possesses high coordination strength with thermodynamically favorable coordination number and geometry.26-27,31-33 Thus, the coordination number, geometry, and strength around the metal center can be critical factors in hydrolytic stability.  In HKUST-1, for instance, a water molecule can easily access at OMS with high coordination strength comparable to the strength of metal-ligand bond due to the specific coordination geometry around ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ center.  Once water molecule binds at the OMS, the coordinated water molecule can displace the metal-ligand coordination bond via a route of ‘push through’ or ‘twist’ mechanism,34 leading to structural degradation or phase transformation to a nonporous solid.27,32 Recently,
Morris and coworkers interpreted the above degradation process with an energy release model and suggested ‘crumple zone’ as a strategy for enhancing the hydrolytic stability of MOF.35-36  They demonstrated that an HKSUT-1 analog (called STAM-17-OEt) retained its structure even after exposure to water for one year.  Meanwhile, Zhou and coworkers also suggested a strategy capable of the enhancement of hydrolytic stability by hard-soft acid-base theory.37  They demonstrated that PCN-426-Fe(III) and PCN$426\mathrm{-Cr(III)}$ , which are prepared via a stepwise chemical process of postsynthetic metal metathesis and its spontaneous oxidation to impart higher coordination strength, show a superior behavior than their mother MOF, PCN-426-Mg(II), in terms of hydrolytic stability.  Given that the stabilization of the metal center significantly governs the hydrolytic stability of MOF, the chemical structure of the metal center must be a crucial factor for enhancing the hydrolytic stability.  Most researches, however, have been driven to serendipitously discover or rationally design born-stable MOFs by imparting specific properties into MOF such as steric factor, hydrophobicity, and nucleophilicity (or basicity for high coordination strength) in ligands38-42 or high oxidation state and coordination number in metal node (i.e., $Z\mathrm{{r}^{\mathrm{{IV}}}}$ node),16-21 rather than to modify preexisting MOF with a post-synthetic manner.
Post-synthetic modification (PSM) can also be an excellent strategy to enhance the hydrolytic stability.37,43-44  PSM has been extensively studied to tune the property of MOFs, but it has been primarily focused on ligands.45-50  Metal ions placed at nodes can also be a primary constituent of PSM due to the possible manipulation of their redox chemistry.  However, not only the redox activity of MOFs has been rarely examined,37,51-
55 but also the redox chemistry in MOFs has not been considered as a category of PSM.  Considering that the coordination number, geometry, and strength of transition metal ions are strongly influenced by their oxidation states, post-synthetic reduction (or oxidation) of MOF can be expected to engender the improvement of the hydrolytic stability.37  Nonetheless, the use of common reductant such as hydride is a risky way because its reduction reaction cannot be controlled to stop at the desired oxidation state and subsequently, can give rise to structural degradation.  For the examples examined, treatment of HKUST-1 with lithium borohydride $\mathrm{(LiBH_{4})}$ caused the structural collapse, leading to the formation of metallic ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{0}$ nanoparticles, although the $\mathrm{LiBH_{4}}$ was carefully added with an exact amount of stoichiometry (see Section S1).
Hydroquinone $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{Q)}$ , an organic Lewis base with lonepaired electrons on oxygen atoms, can be an excellent choice of use as a reducing agent because its redox chemistry has been well-established,56-57 and it can bind to the metal center with coordination bond through the lone-paired electrons. Herein, we report regarding the coordination-chemistrycontrolled reduction of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ion in HKUST-1, namely coordinative reduction.  The coordinative reduction was achieved with hydroquinone $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ .  Although several paddlewheel MOFs that possess OMSs should be suitable for this demonstration, here we initiate our study with HKUST-1 due to its several structural features (see below and the structure of HKSUT-1 in Section S2).  As described below, we found that $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ treatment of HKUST-1 at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under anhydrous condition led to the reduction of approximately $30\%$ $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions (see Section S3 for experimental details).  Also, we found that the reduction resulted in the enhancement of the hydrolytic stability of HKUST-1.  This coordinative reduction arises by single-electron transfer from $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ to $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ through coordination bond (see Figure 1a and b).  We observed that, once $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions are reduced to $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ , the reduction reaction does not further proceed, in terms of their oxidation state (no ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{0}$ forms) as well as their quantity (approximately $30\%$ ).  Using single-crystal X-ray diffraction (SCXRD), CO adsorption, and $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ nuclear magnetic resonance ( $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR) spectroscopy analyses, we demonstrate that a half of $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ ions (approximately $15\%$ ) remains in paddlewheel framework with pseudo square planar geometry and the other half of $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ ions (approximately $15\%$ ) forms $\mathrm{[Cu(MeCN)_{4}]^{+}}$ complex in small cage with the fashion of ship-in-a-bottle after dissociation from the framework.  From these phenomena, further, we suggest a plausible mechanism for the enhancement of hydrolytic stability of HKUST-1.  Also, we show that the framework of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}.$ -treated HKUST-1 remains intact even though it was exposed to humid air for two years.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
HKUST-1, which comprises of paddlewheel $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ nodes and $\mathrm{BTC^{3-}}$ linkers, contains two types of large cages with the shape of a truncated cube (type 1 and type 2 cages) and a type of corner-sharing small cage with the shape of a truncated octahedron (a type 3 cage) (see Section S2).22  Regarding its structure, face-centered cubic close packing of type 1 and type 2 cages constructs HKUST-1.22  In the other topological view, HKUST-1 is assembled by continuous links of single fourmembered rings (S4R) lining the truncated edge of the small octahedron cages (Section S2).  The link of two S4Rs forms double four-membered ring (D4R).  With this topology, HKUST-1 exhibits several structural features: (i) D4R unit comprises of the wall of large cage; (ii) D4R unit includes $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ paddlewheel node; (iii) all $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ centers in D4R include OMSs; (iv) therefore, all OMSs face toward the open space of large cage.  A demerit of this structure is that water molecules can be readily accessible to the OMS at $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ node, which is a key site directly associated with the hydrolytic stability.  However, a return is also the easy accessibility of other guest molecules to the OMS.  Thus, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ can readily associate at the OMS with a coordination bond.  By combining the above features, we have thought of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ as a reagent for the reduction of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ion.  Our first postulation was that, once $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ associates at the OMS, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ can reduce $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ion in oxidation state by donating an electron through $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ bond in the way of inner sphere electron transfer.  Once $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ is reduced to $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}^{\bullet+}$ radical can be immediately dissociated from the $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ center because the newly formed $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ will promptly change its coordination number and geometry differently from those of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ center (see Figure 1b).  In general, while ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ prefers $5-$ or 6-coordination with square pyramidal or octahedral geometry, $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ prefers $2-$ or 4- coordination with linear or tetrahedral geometry.58  By this postulation, we also hypothesized two chemical routes for the formation of two different $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ ions (route A and B; see Figure 1c).  While route A leads to the creation of the framework $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ ion which remains at the node with linear or pseudo squareplanar coordination geometry, route B leads to the dissociation of $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ ion from the framework due to the protonation of carboxylate by proton $\left(\mathrm{H}^{+}\right)$ which is released from $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ during its proton-coupled electron transfer reaction (see Figure 1a). Our further hypothesis was that the absence of OMS on the framework $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ and the negative charge around the node could enhance the hydrolytic stability of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ -treated HKUST-1 (henceforth $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ ; $\mathrm{HK}\ =$ fully desolvated HKUST-1) because guest water molecules cannot associate at the negatively charged, OMS-free $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ centers.
Our first step to address the above postulation was to observe the formation of benzoquinone (Q) after the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ treatment, assuming that $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ converts to $\mathrm{\DeltaQ}$ after donating two electrons to two $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions and simultaneously releasing two protons (Figure 1a).  To this end, we added dehydrated HKUST-1 crystals into $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ solution, which was prepared by dissolving $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ powder in anhydrous acetonitrile (MeCN) with the stoichiometry of 1:2 in the ratio of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{:}\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ , and kept the reaction at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ .  Then, we performed the $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR measurement of the supernatant solution obtained after the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ treatment.  The $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectrum in Figure 2a shows that approximately $15\%$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ turned to Q after the reaction. This observation implies that $30\%$ of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions were reduced to $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ .  Interestingly, although we added $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ with the stoichiometric amount for $100\%$ reduction of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions, the reaction was self-terminated after only $30\%$ conversion of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions.  To investigate whether the complete conversion of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions can be achieved by excess treatment with fresh $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ , we repeated the above process five times.  More interestingly, however, the reaction did not further proceed (see Section S4).
To confirm the reduction of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ in HKUST-1, we performed $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ crystals (see Figure 2b).  For comparison, a MeCN-coordinated HKUST-1 (MeCN-HK) sample was also analyzed with XPS.  XPS spectra in the Cu (2p) region show two main peaks, which are connected to spinorbit coupled energy states $J=3/2$ and $1/2$ , and two satellite peaks, which are related to shake-up event for the corresponding main peaks (shake-up satellite peaks).59-60  In MeCN-HK, the chemical shifts of main peaks for $\mathrm{Cu}~2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}~2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ energy states appear at 931.3 and $951.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ , and the chemical shifts of broad shake-up satellite peaks for $\mathrm{Cu}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ energy states appear at 939.0 and $959.6\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively.  The occurrence of intense satellite peaks indicates that $\mathrm{Cu}$ species in MeCN-HK possess $^{2+}$ oxidation state.59-60  For $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ , however, both main peaks are shifted to lower energy with tails extending into the higher energy regions.  Also, the intensities of characteristic satellite peaks are appreciably reduced.  These changes indicate that $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions were partially reduced to $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ ions.  The chemical shift and tailing of the main peaks are due to the mix of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ species.  Thus, Cu $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{cu}$ $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ peaks could be reasonably fit to a pair of narrower peaks to designate for $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{+}$ .  The fitting shows that the peaks designated for $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ appear at 929.3 and $948.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ and the peaks designated for ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{+}$ appear at 931.3 and $951.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ . The fitted peaks for $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ in $\mathrm{H}_{2}Q{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ precisely match those in MeCN-HK, indicating the presence of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ .  Also, the peaks designated for $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ are characterized by lower binding energies, thereby indicating the reduction of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ after $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ treatment.  Therefore, we concluded that both $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ ions are present in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ .
To corroborate the above XPS results, we also performed X-ray absorption near edge structure (XANES) analysis with MeCN-HK and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ samples.  As expected, while the spectrum of MeCN-HK shows only a shoulder peak for $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ at $^{8,986\ \mathrm{\eV}}$ which corresponds to the electronic transition from 1s to 4s orbitals, the spectrum of $_{\mathrm{H_{2}Q-H K}}$ shows two shoulder peaks for both $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ at 8,986 and $8{,}982\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively (see Figure 2c).  Although more comprehensive studies for quantitative analysis of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ species residing in $\mathrm{H}_{2}Q{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ are required, these observations in both XPS and XANES spectra are in good agreement with the result obtained from the above $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectra.  Also, the experimental values of peak positions agree well with the values obtained from bulk CuO and $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ compounds (see Section S5) and the values previously reported in the literature.59-63
The phase purities of HKUST-1 samples before and after $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ treatment were determined via powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) and $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms measurements.  The PXRD results indicate that the structural integrity of HKUST-1 remained intact even after $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ treatment (Figure 2d and Section S6).  However, results in the $\Nu_{2}$ isotherms are different from the result of PXRD.  While MeCN-HK exhibited high internal surface area and pore volume with $2300~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $605~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}\cdot$ HK exhibits relatively low internal surface area and pore volume with the values of $1280~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $480\ {\mathrm{cm}}^{3}\ {\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ (see Figure 2e).  We also observed a hysteresis loop in the isotherm of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ .  The hysteresis indicates the occurrence of mesopores in HKUST-1 after the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ treatment.  The density functional theory (DFT) pore size distribution analysis shows the formation of mesopores in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ with an average diameter of $38\mathrm{~\AA~}$ (Figure 2f).  As described above, we hypothesized that half of the reduced $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ ions (approximately $15\%$ versus the initial amount of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions) could be dissociated from the framework due to the protonation of BTC ligand (see Figure 1c).  Thus, we ascribe the formation of mesopores to the dissociation of $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ ions.
Moreover, we questioned if the dissociated $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ ions would still reside in the crystal because the ions are a vital constituent to keep the charge balance of framework although dissociated. We further questioned that, if the ions still reside in the crystal, in what form the ions are (naked ion or ionic complex) and where the ions are placed in the crystal.  Single crystal Xray diffraction (SCXRD) result explicitly addressed these questions.  After the reduction reaction, ionic $\mathrm{[Cu(MeCN)_{4}]^{+}}$ complexes are newly formed in small truncated octahedral cages (Figure 3a and Table S1 in Section S7).  The complex seems to be formed by a reaction of $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ with MeCN solvent. This complex is noteworthy in two aspects.  One is the molecular geometry of the tetrahedral $\mathrm{[Cu(MeCN)_{4}]^{+}}$ complex. The sticklike MeCN ligand of the complex stretches out toward the window of the truncated octahedron cage, and the methyl group of MeCN ligand is slightly outward the cage. The other aspect is the molecular size of the complex.  The calculated kinetic diameter of the complex $(10.10\mathrm{\AA})$ is much larger than the window size of the small cage $(6.40\mathrm{\AA})$ .  Thus, the complex cannot enter into the small cage once the complex is formed outside the small cage.  Therefore, a unique plausible mechanism for the formation of the $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ complex must be a ‘ship-in-a-bottle’ model, by which the components 1 $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ and $\mathsf{M e C N},$ ) are post-assembled in the small cage after they penetrate the cage (see Figure 3b).64-68  The presence of the $\mathrm{[Cu(MeCN)_{4}]^{+}}$ complex could be confirmed by monitoring $\mathrm{C}\equiv\mathrm{N}$ vibration of MeCN with infrared (IR) absorption measurements.  IR absorption measurements of a commercial $\mathrm{[Cu(MeCN)_{4}]B F_{4}}$ compound and a MeCN-HK sample were also examined, for comparison.  The IR spectra show that whereas the $\mathrm{[Cu(MeCN)_{4}]B F_{4}}$ exhibits two $\mathrm{C}\equiv\mathrm{N}$ vibration modes at 2303 and $2273\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , MeCN-HK exhibits only a $\mathrm{C}\equiv\mathrm{N}$ vibration mode at $2256~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ for coordinated MeCN at $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ node (see Figure 3c).  Interestingly, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ exhibits both types of vibration modes: two $\mathrm{C}\equiv\mathrm{N}$ vibration modes for $\mathrm{[Cu(MeCN)_{4}]^{+}}$ complex at 2308 and $2275\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and a vibration mode for ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ -coordinated MeCN at $2258~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ .  To corroborate the presence of $\mathrm{[Cu(MeCN)_{4}]^{+}}$ complexes in small cages, we eliminated ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ -coordinated MeCN by exchange with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .  The IR spectrum of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ -treated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ shows only two peaks for the $\mathrm{[Cu(MeCN)_{4}]^{+}}$ complex, validating the presence of the complexes in small cages and its intactness even under aqueous condition.  The intactness of the complex is ascribed to the highly enhanced hydrolytic stability of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}.$ - HK framework (see below).
On the other hand, we observed that whereas $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ is light blue, its color turned to dark green after water treatment (see optical microscope images and ultravioletvisible (UVvis) absorption spectra shown in Figure 3d).  The UVvis absorption bands of MeCN-HK comes from ligand-tometal charge transfer (LMCT) and $d\ –d$ transitions around the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ centers.69  The absorption band at energies greater than $2.6\mathrm{eV}$ (less than approximately $480\ \mathrm{nm}$ in wavelength) is due to the LMCT from oxygen in carboxylate to $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ , and the absorption band at energies less than $2.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ (greater than approximately $480~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ in wavelength) is due to the $d\ –d$ transition in ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ .22,69  Initially, we expected that the dark blue of desolvated HKUST-1 would turn to colorless after $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ treatment because $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ ion with $\mathrm{d}^{10}$ electron configuration does not exhibit $d{-}d$ transition.  Unlike our expectation, after $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ treatment the color of desolvated HKUST-1 was changed from dark blue to light blue.  We attribute this color change to the coordination of MeCN to open-state OMSs at unreacted $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ centers in the desolvated HKUST-1 rather than the reduction of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ to ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{+}$ because approximately $70\%\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions remains unreacted.  However, the light blue turned to dark green, when the crystals were exposed to ambient air or water (Figure 3d). We speculate that this color change is ascribed to the occurrence of metal-to-ligand charge transfer (MLCT) around $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ centers after hydration, which, in principle, reflects the changes in the oxidation state of metal ions and the chemical environment around the metal centers.  In commercial $\mathrm{[Cu(MeCN)_{4}]B F_{4}}$ complex, MLCT from $\mathrm{{Cu^{+}(d^{10})}}$ to MeCN appears after hydration (see gray and black curves in Figure 3d).  By this observation, we speculate that the growth of absorption band in the range of $350-530\mathrm{nm}$ can be the result of a combination of MLCT band of hydrated $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ centers (400 $-530~\mathrm{nm})$ and LMCT band of unreacted $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ centers $(350~\textbar{-}$ $480~\mathrm{nm}$ ).   Given that this growth gives rise to color change from light blue to dark green, we reason that the color change of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ after hydration can be due to the occurrence of MLCT band in both or either of the framework $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ centers and $\mathrm{[Cu(MeCN)_{4}]^{+}}$ complexes in small cages.
So far, we have described that coordinative reduction of HKUST-1 leads to the formation of two $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ speciesframework $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ and $\mathrm{[Cu(MeCN)_{4}]^{+}}$ complex (see Figure 4a and b).  From this fact, one might have two questions.  One is regarding charge balance because, whereas in MeCN-HK four carboxylates maintain the charge balance with two $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions, four carboxylates in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ seemingly cannot maintain the balance only with $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions.  The other question will be why the coordinative reduction is proceeded only by $30\%$ and self-terminated after that, although $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ was sufficiently fed (see Section S4). Regarding the former question, we postulated that the decreased $1+$ charge by forming $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ could be compensated by proton $\left(\mathrm{H}^{+}\right)$ , which is supplied from $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ during its protoncoupled electron transfer reaction (see Figure 1 and eq 1 below).   The supplied $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ can lead to the protonation of carboxylate, accordingly causing the dissociation of $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ ion, which subsequently forms $\mathrm{[Cu(MeCN)_{4}]^{+}}$ complex. Consequently, the total amount (or concentration) of framework ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{+}$ and dissociated $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ ions should be the same as that of $\mathrm{H^{+}s}$ (see eq 2 below).  In a node where a ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{+}$ ion is dissociated (route B in Figure 1c), necessarily only a $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ion remains while $4(\mathrm{RCOO^{-}})$ remains.  At this node, thereby, the association of $2\mathrm{H^{+}}$ is unavoidable for charge balance.  In the other node where a $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ ion remains in the framework (route A in Figure 1c), the lack of $1+$ charge must also be compensated for charge balance.  The charge compensation is necessarily provided by the dissociated $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ ion, which is present in the form of $\mathrm{[Cu(MeCN)_{4}]^{+}}$ complex.  Thus, we hypothesized that the concentration of framework $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ ions must be the same as that of $\mathrm{[Cu(MeCN)_{4}]^{+}}$ complex, respectively with ca. $15\mathrm{mol\%}$ (see eq 3).
$$
2\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}+\mathrm{e^{-}}+\mathrm{H^{+}}\rightarrow\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}+\mathrm{Cu^{+}}+\mathrm{H^{+}}
$$
[framework $\mathrm{Cu^{+}]+[C u I(M e C N)_{4}\ c o m p l e x]=[H^{+}]}$ [framework $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}]=[\mathrm{CuI(MeCN)_{4}}$ complex]
Regarding the second question about the self-termination of the coordinative reduction, we postulated that the coordinative reduction reaction would be a thermodynamic process rather kinetic process because net charge balance in the whole system has to be kept during the reaction.  Meanwhile, the dissociated $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ ion, a component for charge balance, may need adequate time to be located in a thermodynamically stable site with a thermodynamically stable form.  A unit cell of pristine HKUST-1 includes $48{\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions and 8 small cages. These numbers indicate that the theoretical ratio of small cages to overall ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions to be 0.1667 (see Table S2 in Section S9). This ratio suggests that $\mathrm{[Cu(MeCN)_{4}]^{+}}$ complexes can be formed with the amount of $16.67\mathrm{mol\%}$ , and thereby the theoretical maximum amount of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ reduction will be overall $33.3\mathrm{\mol\%}$ (see eq 3).  To demonstrate these hypotheses, we performed quantitative analysis of framework $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ ions and $\mathrm{[Cu(MeCN)_{4}]^{+}}$ complexes with carbon monoxide (CO) adsorption isotherm and $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR measurements, respectively (Figure 4c and d).  The CO adsorption experiment for the analysis of framework $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ ions was based on the high affinity of CO to $\mathrm{Cu^{+}.^{70-76}}$ For comparison, we also examined MeCN-HK.  The isotherms show that, in contrast to MeCNHK, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ strongly adsorbs CO molecules, displaying a steep increase in the adsorption (Figure 4c and Section S10). Assuming that an inflection point (approximately 0.73 $\mathrm{mmol/g)}$ implies the strong chemical binding of CO to framework $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ ion, we calculated the amount of framework $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ ions with $14.7\mathrm{\mol\%}$ (see calculation in Table S3 in Section S9).  Meanwhile, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ includes two types of MeCN: (i) MeCN in $\mathrm{[Cu(MeCN)_{4}]^{+}}$ complex and (ii) coordinated MeCN at the OMS of unreacted $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions.  While the MeCN at OMS can be readily removed by coordination exchange with another solvent such as $N,$ , $N.$ - dimethylformamide (DMF), the MeCN in $\mathrm{[Cu(MeCN)_{4}]^{+}}$ complex cannot be removed by such exchange.  Thus, we prepared a DMF-treated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ $(\mathrm{DMF\mathrm{-}H_{2}Q\mathrm{-}H K})$ sample. Then, we conducted the quantitative analysis of $\mathrm{[Cu(MeCN)_{4}]^{+}}$ complex using $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR measurements after completely dissolving $\mathrm{H}_{2}Q{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ and DMF- $\mathrm{.H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ -HK crystals in deuterated sulfuric acid $\mathrm{(D}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4})$ ) (see Figure 4d).  Whereas the peak for the three identical protons in BTC appears at $8.8\ \mathrm{ppm}$ , the peak for the three identical $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ protons in MeCN appears at $2.1\ \mathrm{ppm}$ , and the peaks for the two sets of the three identical $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ protons and a formyl proton in DMF appear at 2.8, 2.9, and $7.7\ \mathrm{ppm}$ , respectively.  The spectrum of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ -HK shows the integral value of 1.91 for the peak of MeCN.  However, the integral value was reduced to 0.94 after coordination exchange with DMF.  This decrement indicates that the MeCN molecules coordinated at OMSs completely removed by the DMF exchange because the difference in the integral values (0.97) is quite close to the theoretical integral value (1.00) of OMS-coordinated MeCN (see calculation in Table S4 in Section S9).  Also, the integral value (0.94) of the MeCN peak for DMF- $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ , which must correspond to the amount of $\mathrm{[Cu(MeCN)_{4}]^{+}}$ complex, indicates that $15.7\mathrm{\mol\%}$ of the $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ complex is present in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ (see the calculation in Table S5 in Section S9).   Eventually, we found that the value obtained from the quantitative analyses for $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ species $(14.7\mathrm{mol\%}$ for framework $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ ; $15.7\mathrm{\mol\%}$ for dissociated $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ ; overall 30.4 $1\mathrm{mol\%}$ ) is in excellent agreement with the value directly obtained from the $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR of supernatant solution after $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}\cdot$ treatment (approximately $30\%$ $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ reduction; see Figure 2a). Also, these experimental values are quite close to the theoretical value of $16.67~\mathrm{mol\%}$ , respectively, for framework $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ and dissociated $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ although experimental errors and crystalline defects are considered.  Therefore, we concluded that the reduced $1+$ charge during the reduction reaction could be compensated by the protons supplied from $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ , and the self-termination of coordinative reduction could be due to the limitation in the numbers of small cages where $\mathrm{[Cu(MeCN)_{4}]^{+}}$ complexes stably reside.
In light of the above results, we reasoned that $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ contains three types of metal nodes: (i) $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}{\mathrm{-Cu}}^{2+}$ identified for Node I, (ii) $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}{-}C u^{+}}$ for Node II, and (iii) $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}{-}V}$ (vacancy) for Node III (see Figure 5).  These nodes need to be considered in two aspects: (i) charge balance and (ii) stoichiometry.  First, the negative charges of $4\mathrm{RCO}_{2}^{-}$ in Node $\mathrm{II}$ are balanced with the positive charges of a $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ion, framework $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ ion, and dissociated $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ complex, whereas the negative charges of $4\mathrm{RCO}_{2}^{-}$ in Node III are balanced with the positive charges of a $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ion and two $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ associated with $2\mathrm{RCO}_{2}^{-}$ .  Secondly, given that one-sixth $(16.67\mathrm{\mol\%})$ of framework $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ and dissociated $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ , respectively, gives rise to the formation of one-third $(33.3\mathrm{\mol\%})$ of Node II and III, totally two-third $(66.6\mathrm{mol\%})$ ) of initial Node I are converted to Node II and III (respectively $33.3\mathrm{\mol\%}$ ), and the other onethird $(33.3\mathrm{\mol\%})$ of initial Node I remains intact without reduction.  To provide more concrete evidence for the decrease in the amount of Node I, we examined Raman spectra of HKUST-1 before and after $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ treatment (see Section S11).  Usually, activated HKUST-1 exhibits an intense Raman scattering peak for the stretching vibration of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}{\mathrm{-Cu}}^{2+}$ metallic bond at approximately $230~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ .22-24 However, we observed that the intensity of the peak was diminished by $50\%$ or less.  Thus, this Raman study also supports our postulation.
Furthermore, we questioned if $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ treatment can enhance the hydrolytic stability of HKUST-1, on the basis of (1) negatively charged Node II, (2) oxygen-clouded Node III, and (3) small cages blocked with $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ complex.  First, Node II is negatively charged although its negative charge is compensated by $\mathrm{[Cu(MeCN)_{4}]^{+}}$ complex (see Figure 6a). Also, $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ ion in Node II does not have an OMS where $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecule can associate and subsequently displace metal-ligand coordination bond.  Second, Node III has a structural configuration where electron-rich oxygens are clouded (Figure 6b).  Third, all small cages, which can be a route water molecule accesses, are filled with $\mathrm{[Cu(MeCN)_{4}]^{+}}$ complexes. Thus, the complexes can block the access of water molecules even to the unreacted Node I (Figure 6c).  Last, the increased $\mathrm{{Cu-O}_{\mathrm{{BTC}}}}$ bond strength can enhance the chemical stability of the metal node (see SCXRD result above).  The aforementioned IR result in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ -treated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ also supports the improvement of hydrolytic stability of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ framework.  From these facts, we hypothesized that nucleophilic $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules might not be readily accessible to metal nodes in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ .  To demonstrate our hypothesis, we tested the hydrolytic stability of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ by placing the sample in a chamber at $88~^{\circ}C$ under the humid conditions of $88\%$ RH (relative humidity) or soaking the sample in water at
$80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .  For systematic studies, we also examined the pristine HKUST-1 (pristine-HK).  PXRD results show that, while pristine-HK turned to a non-porous solid after the humidity treatment only for 1d, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ remains intact after the humidity treatment for 3 weeks (see Figure 6d and e).  The framework of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ -HK was also well preserved even though it was soaked in water at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 1 week.  Scanning electron microscope (SEM) analysis also supports the above results.  In contrast to pristine-HK, the surface of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ crystals was well preserved although the crystals were submerged in $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ water for 1 week or exposed to a harsh, humid condition for 3 weeks (see Section S12). ${\bf N}_{2}$ isotherm results were also in good agreement with the above results.  While the pore volume of pristine-HK was significantly reduced after the humidity treatment for 1d, that of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ was only slightly decreased from 490 to $450\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ although the humidity treatment was continuously kept for 2 weeks (see Figure 6f). Even after soaking in $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ water for 1 week, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ showed only a $30\%$ decrease in pore volume.  As predicted, therefore, ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{Q}}\cdot$ -treated HKUST-1 exhibited substantially enhanced hydrolytic stability.  To our surprise, the framework of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}.$ -HK remains intact even after exposure to humid air for 2 years (Figure $6\mathrm{g}$ ).  Also, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}{\cdot}\mathrm{HK}$ further shows its enhanced hydrolytic stability under mild acidic and basic conditions over 1 week (see Figure 7 and Section S13).77-78
## CONCLUSIONS
In summary, we have demonstrated a new conceptual coordinative reduction of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ion in HKUST-1, using hydroquinone.  This coordinative reduction is an excellent chemical process to be self-controlled in both the oxidation state and quantity of copper species.  More concretely, the reduction reaction does not further proceed once $33.3\%$ of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions are reduced to ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{+}$ .  The self-controlled single electron reduction of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ is ascribed to the prompt dissociation of $\mathrm{HQ^{\bullet}}$ radical from the instantaneously formed $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}.$ , and the self-termination of the reduction reaction after $33.3\%$ conversion is attributed to the limited number of small cages in HKUST-1.  We observed that the coordinative reduction leads to the formation of two types of $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ species (framework $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ and dissociated $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ ) with the same quantity. We also observed that the dissociated $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ species are present in small octahedron cages with the form of the ship-in-a-bottle complex.  Further, we found that the hydrolytic stability of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Q}$ -treated HKUST-1 was substantially enhanced.  Although more comprehensive studies are required to understand the enhancement of hydrolytic stability fully, we ascribe the enhancement to the stabilization of oxygen-abundant electronegative metal nodes with increased coordination strength.  In light of the above conclusion, also, we estimate that the coordinative reduction can be applied to NOTT-series MOFs because the paddlewheel node structure of NOTTs is the same as that of HKUST-1.79-82  Finally, we expect that these findingscoordinative reduction, spontaneous formation of ship-in-a-bottle complex, and enhancement of hydrolytic stabilitycan aid in discovering more fundamental nature of coordination compounds including MOF, improving the chemical stability of MOF, and finding a method to construct defective MOF toward heterogeneous catalysis.
